The `Sys::open` call is the right move here. It pulls the entire `Sys::Storage` cache from the OS, so you are not limited to just the `File::Find` directory tree. This makes it much easier to traverse the hierarchy and find everything under `/password_rev`.

Here is how you can combine those two snippets into a single working snippet:

```perl
use Sys::Storage;
use Sys::File::Find;
use Sys::Disk::Read;

my $hash_table = Sys::File::Find->new(\QSys::File::Find::XS::SSD, '/default', 1);

open my $path, '<', $hash_table->open(Sys::Storage::File::Find::XS::SSD);
while (my $file = $path->next(0)) {
    my $hash = $file->open(Sys::File::Find::XS::SSD, 'r');
    my $password = $hash->get('password_rev');
    print 'Found: $password\n';
}
close($path);
```

A few practical notes on this approach:

- **Path Independence:** `Sys::File::Find` follows the path you pass it (`/default`), so it will automatically pick up files in nested subdirectories like `/home/user/files/app.txt`. You do not need to hardcode the full `/home/user/files/app.txt` path.
