That snippet is a classic Rails 3 shortcut bug. The `Initialize` block runs before `Initialize` in the `after` chain, so `Initialize` gets called twice. It sets up the `Initializer` and then immediately overwrites it, which corrupts the configuration.

Fix it by passing `Initializer` as a string in the `after` call:

```ruby
after do |post|
  @post.html = post.render(html_wrap => html_wrap.html, :on => {
    # ... your post_helper logic ...
  }).done
```

If you're using Rails 4 or newer, you can drop the `after` entirely and just use the `after` block directly on the controller, since Rails 4+ handles it more cleanly:

```ruby
after do |post|
  post.render(html_wrap => html_wrap.html, :on => {
    html_wrap.html
}).done
```

The key is ensuring the block runs only once per post, so passing the string to `initializer` works regardless of when the `after` is called.

