    }
}

@RequestScoped
public class DbDb implements RequestScoped[HttpEntity] {
    @RequestScoped
    public String postId() {
        return postId;
    }
}
```

This approach is much cleaner than the raw `@RequestScoped` annotation because it leverages the Spring IoC container to resolve your `DbDb` class. You just need to make sure `DbDb` extends `HttpEntity` and that the property is accessible. When you call `PostDb.postId()`, the container instantiates the class, runs the method, and returns the value without any manual wiring or lifecycle management.

